Subject: Mark `volatile`-qualified types with a newtype wrapper

Rust has no spelling for `volatile`, so bindgen drops it at every position C++
can write it. `volatile int` and `int` are both `c_int`; a `volatile` member is
an ordinary field; `volatile int a[3]` is `[c_int; 3]`; and `Holder<volatile
int>` and `Holder<int>` come out as one type, though C++ says they are
different specializations.

The position which does the most damage is a variable. `extern volatile int g`
comes out `pub static mut g: c_int`, byte for byte what a non-volatile `int`
gets. Worse, `extern const volatile int g` comes out `pub static g: c_int` - a
*non-`mut`* static, because `is_const` is read and honoured on its own. An
immutable Rust static is one the compiler may cache or constant-fold reads of,
which is the opposite of what `const volatile` promises, and `const volatile`
is how a read-only hardware status register is declared.

`const` had the same problem and `--use-const-newtype-wrapper` already answers
it: `Type` records `is_const`, read from `clang_isConstQualifiedType`, and
codegen wraps such a type in `__bindgen_marker_Const<T>` for a consumer to read
back. This does the same for `volatile`. `Type` gains an `is_volatile` beside
`is_const`, read from `clang_isVolatileQualifiedType`, and
`--use-volatile-newtype-wrapper` wraps in `__bindgen_marker_Volatile<T>`. Off
by default, so no existing output changes.

`volatile` needs the marker in one place `const` does not. A `const` pointee
survives without one, because `*const T` says it. Rust has no volatile pointer
type, so without the marker nothing about a `volatile` type survives anywhere.

Five details differ from a copy of the `const` path.

`Type::new` does not take the new flag. A second bool beside `is_const` in a
four-argument constructor would be swappable with it at every one of the twelve
call sites, and most of those build a type of bindgen's own - a builtin, a
wrapper, a sub-instantiation - which has no C++ qualifier to report.
`with_volatile` records it at the sites which do read one off a clang type, and
the rest keep the default.

An array is qualified by its element: C++ says an array whose element type is
cv-qualified is itself cv-qualified, and clang reports the qualifier on the
element. `is_volatile` is therefore derived for `CXType_ConstantArray` the same
way `is_const` already is.

A `const volatile` type carries both markers, volatile innermost, so that it
reads back in the order C++ writes it. `strip_const_wrapper` accordingly
becomes `strip_qualifier_wrappers` and peels repeatedly; its one caller wants
the type's own name, and neither marker is part of it.

A pointee needs the marker put back by hand. `*const T` carries a pointee's
constness, and there is no `*volatile T` to carry the other qualifier, so the
pointer arm reads it off the pointee before resolving through type references -
which lands on a node that never had it - and re-applies it to the rendered
pointee, guarding against nesting the same way the template-argument arm does.
Without that, `Holder<volatile S*>` and `Holder<S*>` are one type in the output
even though C++ says they are different specializations.

A `volatile` variable is not lowered to a constant. `volatile int g = 1;` was
emitted as a Rust `const` carrying the value, which fixes it at compile time -
the one outcome the qualifier most clearly forbids - and a marker on the
emitted type could not have undone it. The string case could not even be
marked: it substitutes a `&[u8; N]` for the declared type and says so in a
`TODO` of its own. With the option on, such a variable takes the `extern`
declaration path instead, where the qualifier survives on the type.

One hunk lands on code which is not upstream: the `--represent-std-array`
lowering builds a `Type` of its own and records `is_const` without a
counterpart. Anyone rebasing this onto a tree without that option should drop
that hunk.

One position the marker cannot reach, so `denote_data_member` reports it
instead. A run of bitfields shares one allocation unit and each member of it
survives only as accessors over that unit, so there is no field of the member's
own type for a marker to land on - the accessors' signatures carry it and
nothing else does. `DataMemberInfo` already reports `is_const` for exactly this
reason; it now reports `is_volatile` beside it, read through aliases the same
way.

One known gap, which `--use-const-newtype-wrapper` shares for the same reason:
a qualifier written on an alias rather than on the declaration is resolved
through before the alias is emitted, so `typedef volatile int vi;` loses it,
exactly as `typedef const E CE` does. (`is_volatile_through_aliases`, used by
the data-member report, does see through such an alias; it is the *emitted
type* which cannot.)

